readings.c1s1:
¶
{{smaller block|I love good wine,
As I love health and joy of heart, but ''temperately.''
{{right|''Fletcher'', Wit without Money, iii. 1.}}}}

(''b'') Without over-indulgence in eating, drinking, or the like; abstemiously; soberly.

{{smaller block|God esteems it part of his service if we eat or drink; so it be ''temperately'', and as may best preserve health.
{{right|''Jer. Taylor.''}}}}

(''c'') Without violence or extravagance; dispassionately; calmly; sedately.

{{smaller block|''Temperately'' proceed to what you would
Thus violently redress.
{{right|''Shak.'', Cor., iii. 1. 219.}}}}

'''temperateness''' (tem′pėr-ạ̄t-nes), ''n.'' The state or character of being temperate. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') Moderation; freedom from excess: as, ''temperateness'' of language. (''b'') Due control of the natural appetites or desires; temperance; sobriety. (''c'') Calmness; sedateness; equanimity of mind. (''d'') Freedom from excessive heat or cold: as, the ''temperateness'' of a climate.}}

'''temperative''' (tem′pėr-ạ̄-tiv), ''a.'' [< LL. ''temperativus'', serving to moderate, < L. ''temperare'', temper: see ''temper''.] Having the power or quality of tempering.

'''temperature''' (tem′pėr-ạ̄-t̮ūr), ''n.'' [< OF. ''temperature'' (also *''temperure'', > ME. ''temperure''), F. ''température'' = Pr. ''tempradura'' = Sp. ''templadura'' = Pg. ''temperatura'', ''tempratura'' = It. ''temperatura'', < L. ''temperatura'', due measure, proportion, composition, or quality, temper, temperament, temperature, < ''temperare'', moderate, temper: see ''temper''. Cf. ''temperure''.] 1†. Mixture, or that which is produced by mixture; a compound.

{{smaller block|Made a ''temperature'' of brass and iron together.
{{right|''Holland.''}}
A proper ''temperature'' of fear and love.
{{right|''Abp. Secker.''}}}}

readings.c1s2:
¶
2. Constitution; state; temperament.

{{smaller block|The best composition and ''temperature'' is to have openness in fame and opinion, secrecy in habit.
{{right|''Bacon'', Simulation and Dissimulation (ed. 1887).}}}}

3†. Moderation; freedom from passions or excesses.

{{smaller block|In that proud port which her so goodly graceth . . .
Most goodly ''temperature'' ye may descry.
{{right|''Spenser'', Sonnets, xiii.}}}}

{{smaller block|A difficult thing it is for any man that is rich not to submit his minde and affection vnto his money; and, passing many a Crœsus in wealth, to beare a modest ''temperature'' with Numa. {{right|''Heywood'', Hierarchy of Angels, p. 590.}}}}

4†. Temper, as of metals.

{{smaller block|The due ''temperature'' of stiff steel.
{{right|''Holland'', tr. of Plutarch, p. 95.}}}}

5†. Temperateness; mildness.

{{smaller block|This teritory being 15. myle from the shoare, for pleasantnest of seate, for ''temporature'' of climate, fertility of soyle, and comoditie of the Sea, . . . is not to be excelled by any other whatsoeuer.
{{right|Quoted in ''Capt. John Smith's'' Works, I. 87.}}}}

6. The state of a substance with regard to sensible heat; the degree or intensity of the sensible

readings.c2s1:
¶
{{smaller block|Water boils at a lower ''temperature'' at the top of a mountain than it does at the seashore, and . . . ice melts at the same ''temperature'' in all parts of the world.
{{right|''Clerk Maxwell'', Heat, p. 33.}}}}

{{smaller block|Our sensations of ''temperature'' vary considerably according to the "subjective" ''temperature.''
{{right|''J. Sully'', Outlines of Psychol., p. 167.}}}}

'''7.''' Specifically, the thermal element of weather or climate. {{smaller|If the whole surface of the earth were either land or water, and perfectly homogeneous, there would be the same temperature at every point on the same latitude; but in the case of an entire land surface the difference of temperature between the equator and the pole, and consequently the temperature gradient, would be much greater than in the case of an earth entirely covered by water. In the case of the actual earth with continents and oceans, the temperature gradients between the equator and the pole on the continents are somewhat as they would be in the case of an entire land surface, while on the ocean they are somewhat as on an entire water surface, and consequently the temperature gradients on the former are greater than on the latter; hence there are differences of temperature on the same latitude in different longitudes, and temperature gradients arise between regions of land and regions of water. As a result of these diversifying conditions, the mean sea-level temperature can be expressed as a function of latitude and longitude only by empirical methods, and by utilizing a large mass of observed data. The diminution of temperature with altitude is a further variation that can often be independently treated.}}

'''8.''' In ''physiol.'' and ''pathol.'', the degree of heat of a living body, especially of the human body. It is usually taken, clinically, in the axilla, under the tongue, or in the rectum.

{{smaller block|The pulse, respiration, and ''temperature'' may improve.
{{right|''J. M. Carnochan'', Operative Surgery, p. 398.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|Loath was he to move
From the imprinted couch, and, when he did,
'Twas with slow, languid paces, and face hid
In muffling hands. So ''temper'd,'' out he stray'd.
{{right|''Keats'', Endymion, ii.}}}}

2. In ''music'', noting an instrument, scale, or interval that is tuned in accordance with some other temperament than just or pure temperament, specifically one tuned in equal temperament. See ''temperament'', 5.—'''Tempered clay''', {{smaller|clay prepared for molding by moistening and kneading.—'''Tempered-clay machine''', in ''brick-manuf.'', one of a class of machines by which tempered or moistened clay is molded into bricks or tiles.—'''Tempered glass.''' See ''glass.''}}

'''temperedly''' (tem′pėrd-li), ''adv.'' In a tempered manner.

'''temperer''' (tem′pėr-ėr), ''n.'' [< ''temper'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who or that which tempers, in any sense.

{{smaller block|They are weighed out in quantities of about 30 lbs., which contain from 250,000 to 500,000 needles, and are carried in boxes to the ''temperer.'' {{right|''Ure'', Dict., III. 410.}}}}

{{smaller block|It is the duty of the ''temperer'' to see that sufficient water is let to the clay to soak it.
{{right|''C. T. Davis'', Bricks and Tiles, p. 113.}}}}

'''tempering''' (tem′pėr-ing), ''n.'' [Verbal n. of ''temper'', ''v.''] 1. The process of giving to any metal the desired hardness and elasticity. See ''temper'', 9. Also called ''annealing.''—2. In ''music'', the act, process, or result of tuning an instrument, scale, or interval in accordance with some other temperament than just or pure temperament, especially with equal temperament. See ''temperament'', 5.

readings.c3s1:
¶
'''tempering-furnace''' (tem′pėr-ing-fėr″nās), ''n.'' A furnace adapted for the uniform heating of articles which are to be tempered.

'''tempering-oven''' (tem′pėr-ing-uv″n), ''n.'' In ''glass-manuf.'', an annealing-oven used after the melting-oven.

'''tempering-wheel''' (tem′pėr-ing-hwēl), ''n.'' An apparatus for mixing and tempering clay for use in brick-making, etc. {{smaller|It consists of a heavy cast-iron wheel moving in a circular pit, and so geared that it alternately approaches the central pivot and recedes from it.}}

'''temperoure†''', ''n.'' See ''temperure.''

'''temper-screw''' (tem′pėr-skrö), ''n.'' 1. In ''well-boring'', the connecting-link between the working-beam and the cable, which is let out as fast as the drill penetrates the rock, so as to regulate the play of the jars. {{smaller|When the whole length of the screw is run out, it is disengaged and carried up, so as in a few minutes to be ready for another run. See cut under ''oil-derrick''.}}

2. A set-screw the point of which bears against an object or a bearing, and serves to adjust it. ''E. H. Knight.''

'''temperure†''', ''n.'' [ME., also ''temperoure'', ''temprure'', < OF. *''temperure'', < L. ''temperatura'', due measure, temper, temperature: see ''temperature.''] Tempering; temperament.

{{smaller block|The ''temprure'' of the mortere<br>Was maad of lycour wonder dere.
{{right|''Rom. of the Rose'', l. 4177.}}}}

{{smaller block|An other suche as Arione,<br>Whiche had an harpe of suche ''temprure''<br>. . . that he the bestes wilde<br>Made of his note tame and milde.
{{right|''Gower'', Conf. Amant., Prol. (''Richardson.'')}}}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
'''tempest''' (tem′pest), ''n.'' [< ME. ''tempest'', ''tempeste'', < OF. ''tempeste'', F. ''tempête'' = Pr. ''tempesta'' (< L. as if *''tempesta''; cf. ''tempestus'', adj.) = Sp. ''tempestad'' = Pg. ''tempestade'' = It. ''tempestà'', < L. ''tempesta(t-)s'', time, esp. time with respect to physical conditions, weather, and specifically bad weather, a storm or tempest, hence also commotion, disturbance, < ''tempus'' (''tempor-'', ''tempos-''), time: see ''temporal''<sup>1</sup>.] 1. A very violent storm; an extensive current of wind, rushing with great velocity and violence, and commonly attended with rain, hail, or snow; a furious gale; a hurricane.

{{smaller block|Whan thei in ese wene best to lyve,<br>They ben with ''tempest'' alle fordryve.
{{right|''Rom. of the Rose'', l. 3782.}}}}

{{smaller block|What at first was called a gust, the same<br>Hath now a storm's, anon a ''tempest's'' name.
{{right|''Donne'', The Storm.}}}}

2. A violent tumult or commotion; perturbation; violent agitation: as, a ''tempest'' of the passions; a popular or political ''tempest''.

{{smaller block|The ''tempest'' in my mind<br>Doth from my senses take all feeling else<br>Save what beats there.
{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, iii. 4. 12.}}}}

{{smaller|'''A tempest in a tea-pot''', a great disturbance over a small matter. = '''Syn.''' 1. ''Hurricane'', etc. See ''wind''<sup>2</sup>.}}

'''tempest''' (tem′pest), ''v.'' [< ME. ''tempesten'', < OF. ''tempester'', F. ''tempêter'' = Pr. Sp. ''tempestar'' = Pg. ''tempestear'' = It. ''tempestare'', storm; from the noun.] I. ''trans.'' To disturb violently, as by a tempest; rouse; throw into a state of commotion; agitate.

{{smaller block|''Tempest'' thee noght al croked to redresse,<br>In trust of hir that turneth as a ball.}}

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|{{right|''Chaucer'', Truth, l. 8.}}}}

{{smaller block|Part huge of bulk,
Wallowing unwieldy, enormous in their gait,
''Tempest'' the ocean. {{right|''Milton'', P. L., vii. 412.}}}}

{{smaller block|Your last letters betray a mind . . . ''tempested'' up by a thousand various passions.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Citizen of the World, xlvii.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To descend as a tempest; be tempestuous; storm. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|And, by their excess
Of cold in virtue, and cross heat in vice,
Thunder and ''tempest'' on those learned heads,
Whom Cæsar with such honour doth advance.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Poetaster, v. 1.}}}}

'''tempestarian''' (tem-pes-tā′ri-ạn), ''n.'' A sorcerer who professed to raise tempests by magical arts. ''Bingham'', Antiquities, xvi. 5.

'''tempest-beaten''' (tem′pest-bē″tn), ''a.'' Beaten or disturbed by or as by a tempest.

{{smaller block|In the calm harbour of whose gentle breast
My ''tempest-beaten'' soul may safely rest.
{{right|''Dryden'', Aurengzebe, i. 1.}}}}

'''tempestive†''' (tem′pes-tiv), ''a.'' [< OF. *''tempestif'' = Sp. Pg. It. ''tempestivo'', < L. ''tempestivus'', timely, seasonable, opportune, < ''tempestas'', time: see ''tempest.''] Timely; seasonable.

{{smaller block|This despised and dejected shrub . . . was left standing alone, neither obscured from the comfortable beams of the Sunne, nor couered from the chearefull and ''tempestiue'' showres of the Heauens.
{{right|''Heywood'', Hierarchy of Angels, p. 532.}}}}
